WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.334 --> 00:00:02.501
-Our next guest is
an Emmy-winning actor

00:00:02.501 --> 00:00:04.634
and comedian you know
from his work on "Kenan"

00:00:04.634 --> 00:00:06.067
and "Saturday Night Live."

00:00:06.067 --> 00:00:08.667
He stars in "Bust Down," which
is streaming now on Peacock.

00:00:08.667 --> 00:00:10.200
Let's take a look.

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:12.434
-I'm going down to
that sperm bank

00:00:12.434 --> 00:00:14.834
and I'm going go find
whose nut it is.

00:00:14.834 --> 00:00:17.567
-And I'm going too --
to talk to somebody

00:00:17.567 --> 00:00:19.334
because I'm a customer there
and I need to figure out

00:00:19.334 --> 00:00:21.567
how to check my account balance
online.

00:00:21.567 --> 00:00:24.601
-Please welcome to the show
Chris Redd, everybody!

00:00:24.601 --> 00:00:26.634
♪♪
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:26.634 --> 00:00:36.567
♪♪

00:00:36.567 --> 00:00:38.634
-Wow!
-Welcome to the show!

00:00:38.634 --> 00:00:40.734
-Yeah, man, I'm here.
What's this -- This is tight.

00:00:40.734 --> 00:00:43.601
I'm extra bald today.
-Yeah. Really good.

00:00:43.601 --> 00:00:44.734
And you just walked
down the hall.

00:00:44.734 --> 00:00:46.200
How's "SNL" going for you
right now?

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:47.934
-Oh, it's so good, man.

00:00:47.934 --> 00:00:49.868
Zoe Kravitz is there.
-Very excited.

00:00:49.868 --> 00:00:51.367
I'm very excited for her
to host.

00:00:51.367 --> 00:00:53.767
-Oh, she's amazing
and she's the best.

00:00:53.767 --> 00:00:55.534
And I got a new show coming out,

00:00:55.534 --> 00:00:56.834
so this has been the best week
of my life.

00:00:56.834 --> 00:00:58.767
-You've been very busy.
You've got "Kenan,"

00:00:58.767 --> 00:01:00.801
where you costar with our friend
Kenan Thompson.

00:01:00.801 --> 00:01:02.567
-Yeah.
-And you got "Bust Down."

00:01:02.567 --> 00:01:03.934
Now, for those who don't know,

00:01:03.934 --> 00:01:05.667
what does the "Bust Down"
refer to?

00:01:05.667 --> 00:01:06.767
What does the term mean?

00:01:06.767 --> 00:01:10.267
-Well, a bust down
in the black culture

00:01:10.267 --> 00:01:11.601
has been a lot of things

00:01:11.601 --> 00:01:13.334
to a lot of people depending on
where you're from.

00:01:13.334 --> 00:01:15.834
-Okay.
-So, if you're from the South,

00:01:15.834 --> 00:01:18.534
bust down, like, could mean like
if you got a Rollie

00:01:18.534 --> 00:01:19.534
and you want to put
some diamonds in it,

00:01:19.534 --> 00:01:21.167
you bust down your Rollie.
-Okay.

00:01:21.167 --> 00:01:24.968
-In Chicago growing up, bust
down meant somebody that would

00:01:24.968 --> 00:01:26.734
share their body with
the community...

00:01:26.734 --> 00:01:28.534
-Okay.
-...all the time.

00:01:28.534 --> 00:01:30.968
-Okay.
-In a sexual way.

00:01:30.968 --> 00:01:32.267
-Gotcha.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:32.267 --> 00:01:34.100
Sort of someone
who is philanthropic.

00:01:34.100 --> 00:01:35.167
-Yes.
-Yeah.

00:01:35.167 --> 00:01:37.667
-Ambitious, if you will.
-Ambitious. Sure.

00:01:37.667 --> 00:01:41.100
What was the inspiration
for this show?

00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:44.300
-Well, I got a -- I had
a meeting with a producer,

00:01:44.300 --> 00:01:48.634
and he showed me this old TV
show that was terrible.

00:01:48.634 --> 00:01:51.634
And it was, like, centered
around four friends.

00:01:51.634 --> 00:01:54.067
And he was like, "Do you have
three other friends?"

00:01:54.067 --> 00:01:55.801
I'm like, "I do." And so...

00:01:55.801 --> 00:01:57.200
[ Laughter ]

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:00.968
I took that terrible pilot back
to my friends like,

00:02:00.968 --> 00:02:02.000
"Hey, we should remake this."

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:03.267
And they were like,
"That's trash.

00:02:03.267 --> 00:02:04.667
Why, Chris?"
[ Laughter ]

00:02:04.667 --> 00:02:07.000
And I was like, "Well, if we
just do something together,

00:02:07.000 --> 00:02:09.834
I think we can make it happen."
And after some convincing,

00:02:09.834 --> 00:02:12.767
we just made several different
versions of "Bust Down."

00:02:12.767 --> 00:02:14.033
That was like five years ago
now.

00:02:14.033 --> 00:02:16.868
Today would be our little baby
gets to walk.

00:02:16.868 --> 00:02:18.434
-And so this is four friends --

00:02:18.434 --> 00:02:20.801
and a wonderful actor, Sam Jay,
as well.

00:02:20.801 --> 00:02:22.300
-Yeah.
-Who you obviously knew

00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:24.701
from "SNL," and other friends
that were on the writing staff.

00:02:24.701 --> 00:02:28.667
But you're basically four
friends sharing a menial job.

00:02:28.667 --> 00:02:30.701
And most of it takes place
in this break room

00:02:30.701 --> 00:02:32.067
where you're just talking about
different things.

00:02:32.067 --> 00:02:33.701
-Yeah.
-And you're being ridiculous

00:02:33.701 --> 00:02:35.133
and it's super funny.

00:02:35.133 --> 00:02:37.601
Was menial jobs something
that was in your past

00:02:37.601 --> 00:02:39.934
before you were a comedian?
-Yeah, I've been a dream chaser

00:02:39.934 --> 00:02:42.634
my whole life, so I just
dedicated my whole life

00:02:42.634 --> 00:02:44.133
to being broke until I was 30.

00:02:44.133 --> 00:02:45.601
-Okay.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:45.601 --> 00:02:47.734
-So I had a lot of --
I had a lot of jobs.

00:02:47.734 --> 00:02:49.601
I worked at McDonald's
maybe seven times.

00:02:49.601 --> 00:02:51.767
-Okay.
-It was my first job,

00:02:51.767 --> 00:02:55.400
and I remember I got fired
because I was stealing.

00:02:55.400 --> 00:02:57.868
But they didn't know it
because they couldn't prove it,

00:02:57.868 --> 00:03:00.767
so they just put me on
the toilets and I fired myself.

00:03:00.767 --> 00:03:02.000
-Yeah.

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:03.834
-I was like,
"I'm not washing pee.

00:03:03.834 --> 00:03:06.300
I'm fired!" Like, that's not how
you quit.

00:03:06.300 --> 00:03:08.033
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:08.033 --> 00:03:11.667
-But then my best friend at
the time, two years later,

00:03:11.667 --> 00:03:14.868
became a manager there,
but I had burned so many bridges

00:03:14.868 --> 00:03:16.501
with so many jobs
I couldn't get hired.

00:03:16.501 --> 00:03:18.000
So I would just go there
and crack jokes,

00:03:18.000 --> 00:03:20.100
and that's kind of when I
figured out I was funny

00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:22.367
'cause I would crack jokes
until they gave me food.

00:03:22.367 --> 00:03:25.000
I was like, "Damn, I'm so funny
that they giving me food."

00:03:25.000 --> 00:03:27.567
[ Laughter ]
But really, looking back,

00:03:27.567 --> 00:03:29.767
I'm like, "I think they thought
I was homeless."

00:03:29.767 --> 00:03:32.200
[ Laughter ]

00:03:32.200 --> 00:03:34.234
"Let's feed this poor boy."

00:03:34.234 --> 00:03:35.901
[ Laughter ]

00:03:35.901 --> 00:03:38.734
-Now, you did -- Obviously,
you've had a lot of success

00:03:38.734 --> 00:03:43.033
doing, you know,
rap parodies on "SNL,"

00:03:43.033 --> 00:03:46.701
but you actually tried to do
rap music professionally.

00:03:46.701 --> 00:03:48.934
-100%.
I started writing raps

00:03:48.934 --> 00:03:51.567
when I was 10 years old,
and they were not good.

00:03:51.567 --> 00:03:52.901
-Uh-huh.

00:03:52.901 --> 00:03:55.100
-My first rap was about
killing Barney, and --

00:03:55.100 --> 00:03:56.934
[ Laughter ]

00:03:56.934 --> 00:03:58.567
'Cause I didn't want to clean up
my room, and I wasn't gonna

00:03:58.567 --> 00:04:00.267
have nobody telling me
when to clean up.

00:04:00.267 --> 00:04:01.534
Like, chill.

00:04:01.534 --> 00:04:02.901
[ Laughter ]

00:04:02.901 --> 00:04:05.634
-I think it's
a message 10-year-olds

00:04:05.634 --> 00:04:07.367
would have been open to,
but their parents

00:04:07.367 --> 00:04:08.434
probably wouldn't have
bought for them, yeah.

00:04:08.434 --> 00:04:09.968
-At all, yeah.

00:04:09.968 --> 00:04:12.334
But I remember, like,
going through a ton of, like,

00:04:12.334 --> 00:04:13.434
different rap names.

00:04:13.434 --> 00:04:16.300
At one point,
I went with John Dough,

00:04:16.300 --> 00:04:18.567
meaning John Dough
as in "money."

00:04:18.567 --> 00:04:21.734
But it was hard for people
to remember who I was.

00:04:21.734 --> 00:04:24.501
[ Laughter ]

00:04:24.501 --> 00:04:26.868
So it didn't work out that well.
-Yeah. Yeah.

00:04:26.868 --> 00:04:28.133
It twisted in on itself.
-Yeah.

00:04:28.133 --> 00:04:29.434
I was like, "I'm John Dough."
"Who?"

00:04:29.434 --> 00:04:30.467
"John Dough."
"Who?"

00:04:30.467 --> 00:04:33.133
"John -- Alright, man,
it's Chris."

00:04:33.133 --> 00:04:36.000
-And you also have a
stand-up special coming out

00:04:36.000 --> 00:04:37.767
this year, so you
really have stayed busy.

00:04:37.767 --> 00:04:39.300
And it's called
"Why Am I Like This?"

00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:41.501
"Why Are You Like That?"
What is it called? I'm sorry.

00:04:41.501 --> 00:04:43.033
-It's called
"Why Am I Like This?"

00:04:43.033 --> 00:04:46.100
And I called it that because
everyone's been asking me that

00:04:46.100 --> 00:04:47.367
for as long as I can remember.

00:04:47.367 --> 00:04:51.968
Like, my parents and my pastors
and my teachers

00:04:51.968 --> 00:04:54.767
and friends, girlfriends.

00:04:54.767 --> 00:04:57.334
But I'm on this journey just,
like, trying to figure myself

00:04:57.334 --> 00:04:58.434
out as far as, like,

00:04:58.434 --> 00:05:00.067
my mental health
is concerned and all that,

00:05:00.067 --> 00:05:01.534
and I'm making jokes.

00:05:01.534 --> 00:05:06.534
And I haven't found a funny way
to, like, tease my special,

00:05:06.534 --> 00:05:09.133
so now it just sounds like
I'm doing a TED Talk about

00:05:09.133 --> 00:05:12.133
some very emotional things.

00:05:12.133 --> 00:05:13.434
But it's --
No, but it's great.

00:05:13.434 --> 00:05:15.400
I even touch on being a rapper,
because, you know,

00:05:15.400 --> 00:05:17.167
I took that so serious.

00:05:17.167 --> 00:05:19.567
And I used to be like,
"Yo, this is for all the haters.

00:05:19.567 --> 00:05:21.901
This is for the haters
that never believed in me,

00:05:21.901 --> 00:05:23.667
never believed
I'd make it in rap."

00:05:23.667 --> 00:05:25.901
Y'all were right as hell.

00:05:27.868 --> 00:05:29.400
-You're right.
That almost never happens.

00:05:29.400 --> 00:05:30.567
-Never.

00:05:30.567 --> 00:05:32.601
-In rap songs,
no one ever gives credit to

00:05:32.601 --> 00:05:34.067
the haters for
having been right.

00:05:34.067 --> 00:05:36.901
-Yeah, man, 'cause some haters
are just intuitive.

00:05:36.901 --> 00:05:39.434
-Yeah. They're not haters.
They're knowers.

00:05:39.434 --> 00:05:41.300
-Yeah, they're knowers.

00:05:41.300 --> 00:05:42.701
They see a weakness
and they're like,

00:05:42.701 --> 00:05:44.100
"You're not good at that."

00:05:44.100 --> 00:05:47.801
-Yeah. They are
constructive criticizers.

00:05:47.801 --> 00:05:49.667
-Like, "I was trying to
help you, dawg."

00:05:49.667 --> 00:05:53.601
-Yeah. "I like good rap.
I hated what you were doing."

00:05:53.601 --> 00:05:54.934
Yeah.

00:05:54.934 --> 00:05:57.133
-And I like improvising a lot,

00:05:57.133 --> 00:05:58.434
so I'm going to improvise
on the special.

00:05:58.434 --> 00:05:59.601
So I've been practicing that.

00:05:59.601 --> 00:06:02.200
And one of the things
I've been doing --

00:06:02.200 --> 00:06:03.701
It started with
Black History Month,

00:06:03.701 --> 00:06:05.567
but I just kept it going.

00:06:05.567 --> 00:06:07.601
I do -- I play a game called

00:06:07.601 --> 00:06:10.267
"White person,
give me one black fact."

00:06:11.801 --> 00:06:13.567
Yeah, see? Yeah.

00:06:13.567 --> 00:06:16.934
Just as I said that,
I heard, like,

00:06:16.934 --> 00:06:18.667
a bunch of buttholes tighten up.

00:06:18.667 --> 00:06:21.567
You know what I mean?

00:06:21.567 --> 00:06:22.901
Just like
a symphony of buttholes.

00:06:22.901 --> 00:06:24.634
-If you stood up
right now with a microphone

00:06:24.634 --> 00:06:25.801
and pretended to go
into the audience,

00:06:25.801 --> 00:06:27.100
I think they'd go for the door.

00:06:27.100 --> 00:06:28.501
-Yeah, they'd be like,
"Oh, so -- so -- so -- so --

00:06:28.501 --> 00:06:30.767
so the show is over?"

00:06:30.767 --> 00:06:33.968
But it's so fun, because people
always think too hard

00:06:33.968 --> 00:06:36.601
about it when, like,
there's no rules besides

00:06:36.601 --> 00:06:37.701
give me a black fact.

00:06:37.701 --> 00:06:38.968
So they could really just say,

00:06:38.968 --> 00:06:41.467
"I'm watching a black dude
do comedy right now."

00:06:41.467 --> 00:06:42.868
-That's a fact.
-Yeah.

00:06:42.868 --> 00:06:46.434
But, like, it's so
interesting that if you say

00:06:46.434 --> 00:06:48.667
"black fact," people just get,

00:06:48.667 --> 00:06:50.567
"Oh, I don't know nothing
about nothing."

00:06:50.567 --> 00:06:51.767
You know?

00:06:51.767 --> 00:06:55.000
One dude just yelled,
"Rosa Parks!"

00:06:55.000 --> 00:06:57.400
Uh-huh.

00:06:57.400 --> 00:07:01.767
What else?
"Uh, Rosa Parks!"

00:07:01.767 --> 00:07:03.100
-That's fantastic.

00:07:03.100 --> 00:07:04.734
It must be really fun
doing crowd work like that.

00:07:04.734 --> 00:07:07.934
You also are an excellent
impressionist on "SNL."

00:07:07.934 --> 00:07:10.000
-Thank you, man.
-And I feel like

00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:13.234
it is a gift to comedy,
it's a gift to New York City.

00:07:13.234 --> 00:07:15.267
We right now have a mayor
who loves the job...

00:07:15.267 --> 00:07:16.334
-Oh, yeah.

00:07:16.334 --> 00:07:17.901
-...likes putting himself
out there,

00:07:17.901 --> 00:07:19.734
seems to have a good sense
of humor about himself.

00:07:19.734 --> 00:07:21.834
-100%.
-And you got to impersonate

00:07:21.834 --> 00:07:23.467
our mayor, Eric Adams,
on the show.

00:07:23.467 --> 00:07:24.801
-Yeah, man.

00:07:24.801 --> 00:07:27.501
It was one of my
favorite moments on the show.

00:07:27.501 --> 00:07:29.767
-Got a photo of you here.
And it's -- I mean, you fit --

00:07:29.767 --> 00:07:31.167
You fit the part, man.
I'll tell you that much.

00:07:31.167 --> 00:07:32.934
-Yeah, we're both equally bald.

00:07:32.934 --> 00:07:35.801
And, um, but it was --
it was nice

00:07:35.801 --> 00:07:37.767
because he has the energy
of a lot of my uncles.

00:07:37.767 --> 00:07:41.534
I have a huge family and, like,
you know, to talk like this

00:07:41.534 --> 00:07:44.067
and do that and be like,
"Hey, I'm a fly dude.

00:07:44.067 --> 00:07:45.601
I'm a really fly --

00:07:45.601 --> 00:07:47.567
I'm-a get this city together,
y'all.

00:07:47.567 --> 00:07:48.868
You know what I'm sayin'?

00:07:48.868 --> 00:07:51.133
Like, you know,
I'm from Brooklyn kinda.

00:07:51.133 --> 00:07:53.033
Jersey a little bit, too."

00:07:53.033 --> 00:07:55.801
[ Laughter ]
So, you know, I -- That --

00:07:55.801 --> 00:07:58.100
You know, when it comes
to, like, political impressions,

00:07:58.100 --> 00:07:59.601
there's not a whole lot
of options...

00:07:59.601 --> 00:08:01.167
-Yeah.
-...for a young Black man,

00:08:01.167 --> 00:08:04.067
so when that dude strolled up,
I was like,

00:08:04.067 --> 00:08:06.300
"Yeah, I got him. I got him."

00:08:06.300 --> 00:08:08.501
-I like that he's almost saying
to all of us,

00:08:08.501 --> 00:08:10.667
"I'm going to say three things
that make sense

00:08:10.667 --> 00:08:12.334
and then a thing
that totally doesn't.

00:08:12.334 --> 00:08:13.834
-Right.
-Every time I talk."

00:08:13.834 --> 00:08:15.567
And I think
for most New Yorkers,

00:08:15.567 --> 00:08:17.434
we're like, "I'll take 75%."

00:08:17.434 --> 00:08:19.133
-Oh, you know what I mean?

00:08:19.133 --> 00:08:20.934
That last thing you thought
you was gonna say?

00:08:20.934 --> 00:08:23.234
Hold it.
Hold it for the next time.

00:08:23.234 --> 00:08:25.634
But, I mean,
it's just so funny to me

00:08:25.634 --> 00:08:27.501
because he just says
whatever's on his mind.

00:08:27.501 --> 00:08:28.868
-Yeah.
-He's like, "You know,

00:08:28.868 --> 00:08:31.801
the city is boring right now.

00:08:31.801 --> 00:08:34.400
It's real boring.
You know, come on."

00:08:34.400 --> 00:08:36.767
You know?
I just love that energy.

00:08:36.767 --> 00:08:38.667
-Yeah.
-I just hope he does well.

00:08:38.667 --> 00:08:40.400
-It is really --
It is so much fun

00:08:40.400 --> 00:08:41.868
to have a mayor who just thinks

00:08:41.868 --> 00:08:43.734
we're all, like, being
a little bit lame right now.

00:08:43.734 --> 00:08:45.868
-Yeah, but he is
the coolest dude in the room.

00:08:45.868 --> 00:08:48.367
I mean, the man wears bracelets
that I've never seen before.

00:08:48.367 --> 00:08:51.000
-Yeah, he also --
I think this is a --

00:08:51.000 --> 00:08:53.767
He tweeted after you did --
because I think he is --

00:08:53.767 --> 00:08:55.100
Again, this is
the kind of mayor you want.

00:08:55.100 --> 00:08:57.000
He wants to be impersonated
on "SNL."

00:08:57.000 --> 00:08:58.434
-Yeah, yeah.

00:08:58.434 --> 00:09:00.667
-And he wrote, "Chris Redd
is hilarious and looked great,

00:09:00.667 --> 00:09:03.000
but it's not true that I haven't
been sick in 60 years,"

00:09:03.000 --> 00:09:04.300
which was one of the jokes,

00:09:04.300 --> 00:09:06.033
"which is why I got
vaccinated and boosted."

00:09:06.033 --> 00:09:07.167
-Mm-hmm.

00:09:07.167 --> 00:09:08.534
-"And all New Yorkers
should, as well."

00:09:08.534 --> 00:09:10.601
Perfect example of a mayor
saying what you want to say,

00:09:10.601 --> 00:09:12.667
and then there's one
crazy thing at the end.

00:09:12.667 --> 00:09:15.634
"P.S. -- reddsaidit" --
your handle --

00:09:15.634 --> 00:09:17.400
"P.S. -- reddsaidit,
you forgot my earring."

00:09:17.400 --> 00:09:20.801
So that -- He was upset that you
did not get that on his thing.

00:09:20.801 --> 00:09:22.133
-He called that out.

00:09:22.133 --> 00:09:24.501
Out of all the things I did,
he was like,

00:09:24.501 --> 00:09:27.767
"You didn't get my earring!"

00:09:27.767 --> 00:09:30.100
And then I googled how many
times he's worn an earring,

00:09:30.100 --> 00:09:32.701
and most of the times
he don't get his earring either.

00:09:32.701 --> 00:09:35.534
[ Laughter ]
-I think that's like --

00:09:35.534 --> 00:09:37.400
I think people say that to him
all the time.

00:09:37.400 --> 00:09:39.133
They're like,
"You forgot your earring."

00:09:39.133 --> 00:09:40.534
He's like, "Not just me."

00:09:40.534 --> 00:09:43.200
-[ Laughs ] "Not just me.
He did, too."

00:09:43.200 --> 00:09:45.000
-Hey, thanks for being here.
Have a great show this Saturday.

00:09:45.000 --> 00:09:47.334
And congratulations
on the new show.

00:09:47.334 --> 00:09:48.701
-Thank you.
-Chris Redd!

00:09:48.701 --> 00:09:51.767
"Bust Down" is streaming now
on Peacock.

00:09:51.767 --> 00:09:53.400
And "SNL" is back this week

00:09:53.400 --> 00:09:56.300
with host Zoe Kravitz
and musical guest Rosalía.

